title: Why the daemon's warnings are routed by repository
tags: [[Connection to the daemon]]
kind: rationale
audience: dev
summary: A flush on one repository has no business in the message log of another, nor in any log when no workspace is on that repository.

Each entry of the daemon's diagnostics feed carries a `repo` field. For a long time
no emitter filled it, and the GUI appended every line to every workspace, calling
them all "daemon-wide". With two repositories loaded, the flushes of A filled the
message panel of B, where nothing told them from B's own — which makes the panel
useless as soon as one works on two repositories, and the cause could not be seen
from the GUI: the field existed, it only always looked empty.

So the daemon says which repository a line is about — the watcher and the executor
(a flush's summary, a flush stopped or failed, a watch not placed, the budget spent),
a failed automatic backup — and the GUI routes by it (`crates/gui/src/diagnostics.rs`:
`Line::concerns`).

Three rules follow:

* ''A line about a repository no workspace shows goes nowhere in the GUI'' (user's decision, Nov. 2026). It used to be shown everywhere, on the ground that an error about a repository nobody looks at is the one worth seeing; in practice a loaded repository with no workspace on it — the demonstration one, one borrowed for a sync — filled every message panel with its flushes. The line stays in the daemon's feed (`GET /diagnostics`) and on its stderr (`crates/gui/src/diagnostics.rs`: `recipients`).
* ''A new diagnostic about a repository uses the `_for` variant'' (`diagnostics::warn_for(scope, message, repo)`, and `info_for`, `error_for`) when the repository is at hand.
* ''Deep code uses the plain `warn`/`info`/`error`, inside a repository scope.'' The placement walk, a reconcile's directory listing, a metadata parser, the forest's arbitration know no repository, yet run for one. `diagnostics::in_repo(uuid)` returns a guard under which the plain variants of that thread name the repository; it is entered where the daemon starts working for one — `with_repo` (every request on a repository), the detached tasks of `routes/fs.rs`, the executor's flush, the watcher's ingest thread, `RepoState::warm`, `load_config` and `refresh_watches`. A diagnostic outside every scope is daemon-wide. The guard is not `Send`, so it cannot be held across an `.await`. Work moved to a thread of its own must enter the scope again: a thread-local does not follow it.

Filling the field needed no `API_VERSION` bump: it was already in the JSON, always
`null`; giving it a value changes no shape.
